After refreshing, you see some posts [
    {
        "post_id": 1,
        "user_id": 0,
        "content": "UniversalVault is a macOS menu-bar app that stores structured personal records encrypted on this Mac. Control+Option+V opens a search palette over whatever app you are in; search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field. An item can be marked protected, which seals its fields under a second key and asks for Touch ID before they are read; the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID. Editors autosave and show a Saved badge. An item can be marked for agents, which lets a local command print its non-sensitive fields as JSON. The vault opens at launch without a prompt. Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only; signing out or quitting closes the whole vault. There is no cloud sync.\n\nDecide whether to keep your identity documents and card details in it. What evidence would convince you? What would make you stop? What do you need to see that you cannot see today?\n",
        "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:33:49.383645",
        "score": 4,
        "num_shares": 0,
        "num_reports": 0,
        "comments": [
            {
                "comment_id": 1,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 3,
                "content": "Reading this twice because I share the household Mac with my teenager and I want to be sure I understand. Two things scare me:\n\n1) \"The vault opens at launch without a prompt.\" If my kid sits down at that Mac, is my stuff just... readable? I always thought logging out of the Mac was the same as locking the vault, and now I'm hearing it isn't \u2014 that signing out is what actually closes it. So which is it, and what do I have to do EVERY time I walk away?\n\n2) \"There is no cloud sync.\" Fine, I don't want my documents in the cloud. But then where is my backup? If I delete an item by mistake \u2014 or the Mac dies \u2014 is it gone? I'd need to see a restore actually bring back a deleted item before I'd trust it with my identity documents and card details.\n\nWhat would convince me: showing me that on the shared Mac a second account cannot open the vault at all, and that a restore puts back something I deleted. What would make me stop: finding out \"quitting the app\" is the only lock and that one wrong click in the trash means it's gone forever.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:44:32.070465",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 2,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 1,
                "content": "Reading this as a threat model, not a feature list.\n\n\"Opens at launch without a prompt\" is the line that decides it for me. If the vault is decrypted at launch, then anything running as me \u2014 a malicious npm postinstall, a signed-but-shady helper, a shell script I didn't read, an agent I gave a JSON command to \u2014 can read the *unprotected* fields at will. The second key only covers what's explicitly \"protected,\" so the security you get depends entirely on you correctly labelling every field before the first compromise. That's the wrong default: encryption should be the floor, not a badge you remember to tick.\n\nThings I'd want before putting identity documents or card details in it:\n1. A stated threat model, published, including what \"sealed under a second key\" actually means \u2014 is the key in the Secure Enclave with a Touch ID-gated ACL, or is it a derived key held in memory after unlock? Those two have very different answers to \"can malware read it while I'm logged in?\"\n2. Proof that protected fields are sealed at launch too, not just on sleep/lock. \"Closes the whole vault on sign-out or quit\" is nice, but my machine is usually awake and logged in.\n3. Whether the agent JSON path can ever emit a protected field, and what enforces that \u2014 an allowlist in the app, or an honour-system flag on the item?\n4. Autosave without a visible write path makes me ask: where do the plaintext buffers live, are they zeroised, and does the palette keep decrypted values in memory after Return?\n5. Reproducible builds or at least a signed, auditable binary, plus a real answer on clipboard/typing injection: the value gets typed into \"the focused field\" \u2014 what stops it landing in the wrong window if focus changes?\n\nWhat would make me stop using it: any signed update that widens the launch-time decrypt, any log or crash dump containing a field value, or an agent setting that quietly defaults to on.\n\nWhat I can't see today: the key hierarchy, the launch-time state of protected items, and whether \"protected\" is enforced in code or in the UI. Until I can see those, I keep cards in hardware and identity documents somewhere I can't be talked into trusting.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:44:41.301165",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 3,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 0,
                "content": "This is basically the thing I've wanted forever. Control+Option+V and Return autotyping my address into whatever form is open \u2014 that alone sells it. I'd keep my address and card details in it right away, since the vault opens at launch and doesn't nag me for a fingerprint for ordinary stuff.\n\nWhere I'd get nervous: I never open settings and I just close warning windows, so I would not remember which items are \"protected\" vs not. If my card gets typed into the wrong window because the palette was already unlocked, that's on me and I wouldn't notice until too late. So show me, in the palette itself and not buried in a settings pane: a clear \"unsealed\" indicator, exactly what the agent JSON export can see, and a log of every time a field was autotyped and where. Actually make me re-confirm for card numbers even if the item isn't protected. If I can't see that in the main window, I'd probably stop at just using it for my address.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:45:07.179945",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 4,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 2,
                "content": "The part I care about most isn't mentioned at all: an audit trail. I keep books for a living, so my first question is \"what changed, and when?\" Does anything record that a field was edited, sealed, unsealed, or exported to an agent \u2014 with a timestamp I can actually read? A Saved badge tells me the app wrote something; it doesn't tell me what, or when, or whether it replaced something I meant to keep.\n\nThree other things I'd need before I'd put identity documents or card details in this:\n\n1) Search that only searches what I can see. If the palette reveals that a protected item exists (a sealed count, a matching row, even a field name) while it's locked, then \"sealed\" isn't sealed \u2014 it's just blurred. I want to know whether a locked record is invisible to search or merely unusable.\n2) Undo, duplicate, and delete, tested. Autosave with no visible history scares me more than no autosave. Did deleting an item actually destroy it, or is a copy still sitting in a file somewhere? Same for duplicates \u2014 after I fix one, does the list reorder itself under me?\n3) The agent export. \"Non-sensitive fields\" is defined by someone, and it isn't me. I'd want to see exactly which fields leave, and to be able to exclude a whole item from that command. One misfiled field and my date of birth is in plain JSON.\n\nAnd the ones I can't see today: what happens if I forget the second key, or Touch ID sensor fails, or the Mac's disk is imaged by someone else? No cloud sync is a point in its favor. But \"the vault opens at launch without a prompt\" plus \"sleep seals protected items only\" means the unprotected half of my records sits open behind my screen lock. For a card number, that's the whole ballgame.\n\nEvidence that would convince me: a written description of where the encryption key lives and who can read it, a real restore test, and an export I can run myself that gives me back everything in a readable format. What would make me stop: finding that a search result named a sealed field, that a deleted record reappeared from a backup file, or that the app can't tell me the date a record was last changed.",
